    summary: 'A reward model is usually the SFT (or base) model with the LM head replaced by a scalar head, trained so that `r(x, y_w) > r(x, y_l)` on preference pairs. Under the Bradley-Terry assumption the loss is the negative log-sigmoid of the reward difference. The RM then supplies the reward signal for RLHF and is a common tool for filtering and best-of-n selection.',
    purpose: 'Turns sparse human comparisons into a dense, reusable scoring function that can evaluate arbitrary new samples during RL.',
    mechanics: 'The model reads the templated prompt and response and outputs one scalar, typically taken from the final hidden state at the last token (or the end-of-turn token) through a linear head. The Bradley-Terry model posits `P(y_w ≻ y_l | x) = σ(r(x, y_w) − r(x, y_l))`, so training minimizes `−log σ(r(x, y_w) − r(x, y_l))` over pairs; a margin term or a small penalty on reward magnitude is sometimes added to keep scores centered. Both responses of a pair are run in the same batch so their scores are compared under identical conditions. Held-out pair accuracy (fraction of pairs where the RM prefers the chosen response) is the main quality metric; human agreement ceilings are typically well below 100 percent, so RM accuracy is bounded accordingly. Reward models are exploited by RL policies when they extrapolate badly outside the training distribution, which is why RM ensembles, periodic RM refreshes on fresh on-policy pairs, and KL penalties are used together. Variants include process reward models that score intermediate steps and rule-based or verifiable rewards (unit tests, exact answers) that bypass the learned RM for checkable tasks.',
