    mechanics: "Four models are typically involved: the policy `π_θ` (trained), the reference `π_ref` (frozen), the reward model `r`, and, for PPO, a value model `V_φ`. Each step samples a batch of prompts, generates responses from `π_θ`, and computes a per-sequence reward `R = r(x, y) − β·KL(π_θ(·|x) ‖ π_ref(·|x))`, where the KL term is usually estimated per token from `log π_θ(y_t) − log π_ref(y_t)` and `β` controls how far the policy may drift. Advantages are estimated with GAE from the value model, and the policy is updated on the clipped surrogate `L = E[min(ρ_t A_t, clip(ρ_t, 1−ε, 1+ε) A_t)]` with `ρ_t = π_θ(y_t|·) / π_old(y_t|·)`, for a few epochs over the same rollouts. Generation dominates wall-clock, so rollouts run on an inference engine (for example vLLM) with weights synchronized from the trainer each step. GRPO, as described in the DeepSeekMath paper, drops the value model: for each prompt it samples a group of `G` responses and sets each advantage to the group-normalized reward `(r_i − mean(r)) / std(r)`, with the KL term added to the loss; this reduces memory and is common with verifiable rewards. Reward hacking is monitored by tracking KL, response length, and periodic human or held-out judge evaluations, since RM score alone will keep rising even as true quality falls.",
